\title{Controlled Failure of Associativity: Thinness, Coherence, and a Catalan Census of Rebracketing}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study categorical structures in which the associativity of composition (or of
a tensor product) is not required to hold on the nose, but only up to a
distinguished invertible comparison, the \emph{associator}. We isolate a simple
hypothesis --- \textbf{thinness}, the requirement that between any two objects
there be at most one morphism --- and show that under it Mac Lane's coherence
conditions (the pentagon and triangle identities, together with all naturality
squares) are satisfied automatically by \emph{any} choice of tensor data. We
then exhibit an explicit model: the \textbf{parenthesization category}
$\PTree(\alpha)$, whose objects are fully-bracketed words over an alphabet
$\alpha$ and whose morphisms record agreement of underlying words. This category
is a genuine non-strict monoidal category --- its tensor product fails to be
associative on objects, provably so by a size argument --- yet it is coherent for
free, its associators are unique, and its skeleton is the free monoid
$(\mathrm{List}\,\alpha, +\!+, [\,])$. Finally, we give a combinatorial census
of the reassociation groupoid: the connected components are the words, and the
size of the component built from $n+1$ factors is the Catalan number $C_n$,
satisfying the Segner convolution recurrence. Together these results characterize
a \emph{loop-tolerant} associativity as equivalent data to a strict monoid
decorated by a Catalan-indexed bookkeeping of bracketings.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem of grouping}
Associativity --- the identity $(a\cdot b)\cdot c = a\cdot(b\cdot c)$ --- is the
law that lets us drop parentheses from a chain of operations. In elementary
algebra it holds strictly and is quickly forgotten. In higher algebra and
category theory, however, the natural comparisons between differently-grouped
products are frequently \emph{isomorphisms} rather than \emph{equalities}. One
keeps the brackets and supplies, for every triple of objects $A,B,C$, a
distinguished invertible morphism
\[
\alpha_{A,B,C} \colon (A \otimes B)\otimes C \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ } A\otimes(B\otimes C),
\]
the \textbf{associator}. This is the setting of monoidal categories, and the
associator is exactly the datum witnessing that ``composition loops back'':
different bracketings of the same product are reversibly identified, but not
equated.

Allowing invertible comparisons in place of equalities raises the question of
\textbf{coherence}. Different sequences of associator moves connecting the same
pair of bracketings must land on the same isomorphism. Mac Lane's coherence
theorem singles out two diagrams whose commutativity suffices for all such
diagrams to commute: the \textbf{pentagon}, relating the five bracketings of a
quadruple product, and the \textbf{triangle}, relating the associator to the
unit isomorphisms.

\subsection{Contributions}
This paper develops three results, from abstract sufficiency to concrete model to
combinatorial measurement.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \emph{Free coherence from thinness} (Section~\ref{sec:thin}). If a
  category is \emph{thin} --- any two parallel morphisms are equal --- then any
  tensor data on it satisfies the pentagon, triangle, and all naturality
  conditions automatically.
  \item \emph{A concrete non-strict monoidal category}
  (Section~\ref{sec:ptree}). The parenthesization category $\PTree(\alpha)$
  realizes controlled non-associativity explicitly: its tensor product is
  genuinely non-associative on objects, yet it is monoidal with unique
  associators, and its skeleton is the free monoid.
  \item \emph{A Catalan census of the loops} (Section~\ref{sec:census}). The
  connected components of the reassociation groupoid are the underlying words;
  the component of a product of $n+1$ factors has cardinality $C_n$, obeying the
  Segner convolution recurrence.
\end{enumerate}
The unifying slogan: a loop-tolerant (coherent, non-strict) associative structure
carries exactly the information of a strict monoid together with a
Catalan-indexed record of how bracketings are glued.

\section{Preliminaries}

We work with ordinary categories. A morphism $f$ is an \emph{isomorphism} if it
has a two-sided inverse; we write $X\cong Y$ when one exists. A \emph{groupoid}
is a category in which every morphism is an isomorphism.

A \emph{monoidal structure} on $\mathcal{C}$ consists of a tensor product
$\otimes$, a unit $\unit$, a natural associator
$\alpha_{A,B,C}\colon (A\otimes B)\otimes C\cong A\otimes(B\otimes C)$, and
natural unitors $\lambda_A\colon \unit\otimes A\cong A$,
$\rho_A\colon A\otimes\unit\cong A$. It is a \emph{monoidal category} if it
satisfies the \textbf{pentagon} identity
\[
(\alpha_{W,X,Y}\otimes \id_Z)\, ;\, \alpha_{W, X\otimes Y, Z}\, ;\, (\id_W\otimes \alpha_{X,Y,Z})
= \alpha_{W\otimes X, Y, Z}\, ;\, \alpha_{W, X, Y\otimes Z}
\]
and the \textbf{triangle} identity
$\alpha_{X,\unit,Y}\, ;\, (\id_X\otimes \lambda_Y) = \rho_X \otimes \id_Y$.
Here $f\, ;\, g$ denotes left-to-right composition. A monoidal category is
\emph{strict} if $\alpha,\lambda,\rho$ are identities, and \emph{non-strict}
otherwise. Mac Lane's coherence theorem asserts that in any monoidal category
every formal diagram of associators and unitors commutes.

\section{Coherence is free in a thin category}\label{sec:thin}

\begin{definition}[Thin category]
A category $\mathcal{C}$ is \emph{thin} if for any objects $X,Y$ and any parallel
morphisms $f,g\colon X\to Y$ one has $f=g$. Equivalently, each hom-set is a
subsingleton and $\mathcal{C}$ is a preorder.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness of morphisms and isomorphisms]\label{lem:unique}
In a thin category, each hom-set has at most one element, and the class of
isomorphisms $X\cong Y$ has at most one element.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The first claim is the definition. For the second, two isomorphisms
$\varphi,\psi\colon X\cong Y$ have equal underlying morphisms by thinness, and an
isomorphism is determined by its underlying morphism; hence $\varphi=\psi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coherence is free in a thin category]\label{thm:free}
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a thin category equipped with any monoidal-structure data.
Then $\mathcal{C}$ is a monoidal category: the pentagon identity, the triangle
identity, and every naturality condition hold automatically.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each required identity is an equation between two morphisms with a common source
and target. Any two such parallel morphisms are equal by thinness. Hence every
coherence and naturality equation holds. 
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A causal loop closes to the identity]
In any monoidal structure on a thin category, the composite of the long Mac Lane
route around the pentagon followed by the inverse of the short route equals the
identity morphism.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both composites are endomorphisms of a fixed object; by thinness the composite
equals $\id$.
\end{proof}

\section{The parenthesization category}\label{sec:ptree}

Fix an alphabet $\alpha$.

\begin{definition}[Parenthesization trees]
Let $\PTree(\alpha)$ be the binary trees with leaves labelled in $\alpha$,
generated by $\nil$, $\leaf(a)$, and $\nodee(s,t)$ (read as $(s\cdot t)$). The
\emph{flattening} forgets brackets:
$\flatop(\nil)=[\,]$, $\flatop(\leaf\,a)=[a]$,
$\flatop(\nodee(s,t))=\flatop(s)+\!+\flatop(t)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The parenthesization category]
Make $\PTree(\alpha)$ a category with $\mathrm{Hom}(s,t)$ the set of proofs that
$\flatop(s)=\flatop(t)$ (at most one element); the identity is reflexivity and
composition is transitivity of equality.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\PTree(\alpha)$ is a thin groupoid.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Any two morphisms $s\to t$ prove the same word-equality, hence coincide
(thinness). The symmetric proof inverts any morphism, and both composites lie in
singleton hom-sets, hence are identities.
\end{proof}

We define tensor data: $s\otimes t = \nodee(s,t)$; unit $\unit=\nil$; associator
from associativity of concatenation; unitors from $[\,]+\!+w = w = w+\!+[\,]$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:free} these form a monoidal category.

\begin{theorem}[Associativity fails on the nose]\label{thm:notassoc}
For all $a,b,c$ the objects $(a\otimes b)\otimes c = \nodee(\nodee(a,b),c)$ and
$a\otimes(b\otimes c) = \nodee(a,\nodee(b,c))$ are distinct. Hence
$\PTree(\alpha)$ is genuinely non-strict (already at $a=b=c=\nil$).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\nodee(\nodee(a,b),c) = \nodee(a,\nodee(b,c))$ then matching left children
gives $\nodee(a,b)=a$, impossible by a structural-size count.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the associator; coherence is connectedness]\label{thm:isoiff}
For all $a,b,c$ the associator is the unique isomorphism
$(a\otimes b)\otimes c \cong a\otimes(b\otimes c)$. More generally,
$s \cong t \iff \flatop(s)=\flatop(t)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Uniqueness is Lemma~\ref{lem:unique}. An isomorphism $s\cong t$ is a morphism,
i.e.\ a proof $\flatop(s)=\flatop(t)$; conversely such a proof yields the
isomorphism.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Normal form]
For $w=[a_1,\dots,a_k]$ let $\ofList(w)$ be the right-nested bracketing, with
$\ofList([\,])=\nil$. Then $\flatop(\ofList(w))=w$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Strictification]
(a) Every object satisfies $s\cong \ofList(\flatop(s))$.
(b) $\ofList(w_1)\otimes \ofList(w_2)\cong \ofList(w_1+\!+w_2)$ with unit
$\ofList([\,])=\unit$. Hence the skeleton of $\PTree(\alpha)$ is the free monoid
$(\mathrm{List}(\alpha),+\!+,[\,])$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both parts follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:isoiff} since the relevant objects
flatten to equal words; the induced operation on isomorphism classes is
concatenation, the free-monoid multiplication.
\end{proof}

\section{A combinatorial census of the loops}\label{sec:census}

\begin{definition}[Shapes]
A \emph{bracketing shape} is a binary tree: $\lf$ (a factor) or $\br(l,r)$ (a
product). Set $\leaves(\lf)=1$, $\leaves(\br(l,r))=\leaves(l)+\leaves(r)$; and
$\prodn(\lf)=0$, $\prodn(\br(l,r))=\prodn(l)+\prodn(r)+1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
For every shape $s$, $\leaves(s)=\prodn(s)+1$: a product of $n+1$ factors uses
exactly $n$ brackets.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Shapes are finite binary trees]\label{prop:treeeq}
Sending $\lf$ to the empty tree and $\br(l,r)$ to an internal node with subtrees
the images of $l,r$ is a bijection between bracketing shapes and finite binary
trees, under which products correspond to internal nodes.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The evident inverse and structural induction give mutually inverse maps; the
node-count identity is a further induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
Let $\Brk(n)$ be the finite set of bracketing shapes with exactly $n$ products
(equivalently $n+1$ factors), obtained by transporting the binary trees with $n$
internal nodes across Proposition~\ref{prop:treeeq}. Then $s\in\Brk(n)$ iff
$\prodn(s)=n$.
\end{definition}

Recall the Catalan numbers $C_0=1$, $C_{n+1}=\sum_{i=0}^{n}C_iC_{n-i}$, i.e.\
$C_n=\frac{1}{n+1}\binom{2n}{n}$: $1,1,2,5,14,42,132,\dots$.

\begin{theorem}[The census of loops]\label{thm:census}
$|\Brk(n)| = C_n$. Equivalently, each connected component of the reassociation
groupoid --- the bracketings of one word of length $n+1$ --- has exactly $C_n$
members, all mutually and uniquely isomorphic.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:treeeq}, $\Brk(n)$ is in bijection with the binary trees
having $n$ internal nodes, whose count is the Catalan number $C_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Segner convolution for the census]
$|\Brk(n+1)| = \sum_{i=0}^{n} |\Brk(i)|\cdot|\Brk(n-i)|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:census} into the Segner recurrence; combinatorially,
split each bracketing at its outermost bracket and sum over the split point.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Realization]
The map $\real$ sending $\lf\mapsto\leaf(\ast)$ and
$\br(l,r)\mapsto\nodee(\real(l),\real(r))$ into $\PTree(\{\ast\})$ is injective,
and $\flatop(\real(s))$ is $\ast$ repeated $\leaves(s)$ times.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Both statements are structural inductions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Components are isomorphism classes]
If $\leaves(s)=\leaves(t)$ then $\real(s)\cong\real(t)$. Hence the $C_n$
bracketings of $n+1$ factors form a single connected component of the
reassociation groupoid, of size $C_n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both realizations flatten to the same repeated word, so they are isomorphic by
Theorem~\ref{thm:isoiff}; uniqueness is Lemma~\ref{lem:unique}.
\end{proof}

\section{Applications and future directions}

In categorical models of quantum processes the associator stores the information
of composition order, and the pentagon is the consistency law; free coherence
identifies a broad regime (thin comparison data) where consistency is automatic.
Bracketings are parse trees, so $C_n$ counts the parse trees of an $n+1$-ary
associative operator and the normal-form map is its canonicalization.

We conjecture: (i) the directed reassociation moves assemble into the
associahedron $K_n$ with Tamari face lattice; (ii) thinness is not only
sufficient but necessary for free coherence; (iii) $k$-ary products yield the
Fuss--Catalan census $C^{(k)}_n=\frac{1}{(k-1)n+1}\binom{kn}{n}$, recovering the
binary case at $k=2$.

\section{Conclusion}

A controlled failure of associativity is best understood through three linked
facts: in a thin world coherence is free; the parenthesization category realizes
this concretely as a genuinely non-strict monoidal category whose skeleton is the
free monoid; and the reassociation groupoid's components are the words, each of
size the Catalan number $C_n$, obeying the Segner convolution. Loop-tolerant
associativity is thus equivalent data to a strict monoid together with a
Catalan-indexed bookkeeping of bracketings.

